<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · song</p><h1 class="enc-title">A las Barricadas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">early 1930s</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">&quot;A las Barricadas&quot; (&quot;To the Barricades&quot;) is a Spanish anarchist anthem. The Spanish anarchist writer Valeriano Orobón Fernández wrote the words in the early 1930s, basing them on a German translation of the Polish revolutionary song known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warszawianka">Warszawianka</a>, whose Polish words Wacław Święcicki had written in the early 1880s and whose music Józef Pławiński had composed.</p></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>&quot;<b>A las Barricadas</b>&quot; (&quot;To the Barricades&quot;) is a Spanish anarchist anthem.</blockquote><a href="/songs/a-las-barricadas">A las Barricadas</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1935 the Falangists wanted a stirring song of their own to answer the &quot;Himno de Riego&quot;, the official anthem of the Second Spanish Republic, and &quot;<b>A las Barricadas</b>&quot;, a very popular anarchist song.</blockquote><a href="/songs/cara-al-sol">Cara al Sol</a></li></ul></section>
